<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Schweinfurt</h1><p class="st-meta">1254 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Schweinfurt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Imperial city on the Main destroyed in the margrave war of 1554 and birthplace of the Leopoldina, Germany's oldest academy of sciences.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Schweinfurt grew around a crossing of the Main and was rebuilt after destruction in the 13th century, being recognised as an imperial city around 1254. It lay between the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> and had to defend its freedom against both.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ruled, and the town lived from trade on the Main, crafts and wine. It adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1542. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-margrave-war">Second Margrave War</a> Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-alcibiades-margrave-of-brandenburg-kulmbach">Albert Alcibiades</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Brandenburg-Kulmbach</a> occupied it, and in 1554 his enemies destroyed the city. In 1652 four Schweinfurt physicians founded the academy later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldina">Leopoldina</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Schweinfurt lost its freedom in 1802 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> annexed it. In the 19th century it became an industrial town known for ball bearings, and it is now in Bavaria. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldina">Leopoldina</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> national academy of sciences.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1254 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.66%;width:10.91%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1254 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schweinfurt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schweinfurt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1528&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-schweinfurt" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Schweinfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4914 437 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Schweinfurt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5234 693L5229 694M5234 693L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5234 693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5234 693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schweinfurt. <a href="/atlas?year=1528&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-schweinfurt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
